1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:03,750
The night of the twenty-eighth of May, 1453.

2
00:00:03,830 --> 00:00:11,012
A young man of twenty-one
sits on his horse before

3
00:00:11,062 --> 00:00:17,570
the greatest walls ever
built by human hands.

4
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:19,364
Behind them lies a city that
has stood for a thousand years,

5
00:00:19,414 --> 00:00:21,120
turning armies from its gates
generation after generation.

6
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:22,240
And in a few hours…

7
00:00:22,290 --> 00:00:24,400
the face of history will change.

8
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:33,200
THE CONQUEROR
Mehmed II and the fall of Constantinople, 1453

9
00:00:36,850 --> 00:00:43,177
Emperor Constantine founded it where
two continents and two seas meet,

10
00:00:43,227 --> 00:00:49,330
and it became the capital of the
Romans and the heart of the world.

11
00:00:49,410 --> 00:00:54,170
The Arabs besieged it twice
under the Umayyads, and many

12
00:00:54,220 --> 00:00:59,460
others tried after them; all
were thrown back from its walls.

13
00:00:59,540 --> 00:01:08,190
Until people came to say:
this city does not fall.

14
00:01:08,270 --> 00:01:10,555
Its land walls ran for about six kilometres,
and the sea guarded every other side,

15
00:01:10,605 --> 00:01:12,000
as if nature had built a
fortress before men did.

16
00:01:13,450 --> 00:01:17,639
Fifty years earlier the city had
nearly fallen to the Ottomans,

17
00:01:17,689 --> 00:01:23,340
had Timur not defeated Bayezid at Ankara
and plunged his sons into a long civil war.

18
00:01:23,420 --> 00:01:27,020
And now, Bayezid's
great-great-grandson stands before it…

19
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:33,533
to finish what his forefathers began.

20
00:01:34,983 --> 00:01:42,373
Mehmed, son of Murad II,
was born in Edirne in 1432.

21
00:01:42,453 --> 00:01:46,783
At twelve, his father gave
up the throne to him.

22
00:01:46,863 --> 00:01:54,143
But a crusade marched east, and Murad
returned to lead the army at Varna, and won.

23
00:01:54,223 --> 00:01:57,424
Then, through the grand vizier Halil Pasha,

24
00:01:57,474 --> 00:02:01,933
Murad took back the throne and
sent the boy away to Manisa.

25
00:02:03,383 --> 00:02:11,373
There, in the years of
exile, the boy made himself.

26
00:02:11,453 --> 00:02:15,828
He learned Arabic, Persian and Greek, read
history, geometry and the art of war,

27
00:02:15,878 --> 00:02:19,473
and studied under scholars, the
foremost among them Akshamsaddin.

28
00:02:19,553 --> 00:02:24,207
It is said his teacher showed
him the city's walls on maps,

29
00:02:24,257 --> 00:02:31,033
and told him of the hadith people repeated:
Constantinople shall surely be conquered.

30
00:02:31,113 --> 00:02:35,697
He drew siege plans with his
own hand and asked about every

31
00:02:35,747 --> 00:02:40,067
tower and every gate, as if
he knew his day was coming.

32
00:02:41,517 --> 00:02:51,647
In February 1451 Murad II died, and
Mehmed took the throne at nineteen.

33
00:02:51,727 --> 00:02:55,467
Europe breathed a sigh of relief; they saw
an inexperienced youth, no one to fear.

34
00:02:55,547 --> 00:02:59,757
And that was…

35
00:02:59,837 --> 00:03:02,500
their greatest mistake.

36
00:03:03,950 --> 00:03:10,617
In Constantinople ruled Constantine
XI, last emperor of the Romans,

37
00:03:10,667 --> 00:03:15,580
over a city whose empire
had shrunk to its walls.

38
00:03:15,660 --> 00:03:19,876
He wanted to test the new
sultan: he demanded money,

39
00:03:19,926 --> 00:03:26,980
and threatened to release an Ottoman prince
living at his court to contest the throne.

40
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:29,300
That became the pretext.

41
00:03:30,750 --> 00:03:33,550
Mehmed answered with stone, not words.

42
00:03:33,630 --> 00:03:41,810
In the spring of 1452 he began a huge fortress
on the European shore of the Bosphorus,

43
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:49,420
at its narrowest point, facing a castle
built by his great-grandfather Bayezid.

44
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:55,730
In about four and a half
months, Rumeli Hisarı rose…

45
00:03:55,810 --> 00:03:59,600
and people called it:
the Cutter of the Strait.

46
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:03,171
The strait was in his grip,
and the wheat and aid that

47
00:04:03,221 --> 00:04:06,433
reached the city from the
Black Sea were cut off.

48
00:04:07,883 --> 00:04:13,303
He declared that every ship passing
the strait must stop to be searched.

49
00:04:13,383 --> 00:04:18,083
When a Venetian ship tried to pass
without leave, the fortress guns sank it.

50
00:04:18,163 --> 00:04:25,033
Europe finally understood the message:
this young man was not playing.

51
00:04:26,483 --> 00:04:32,561
In those days a Hungarian engineer
named Orban came to the emperor,

52
00:04:32,611 --> 00:04:37,183
offering to cast cannons
the world had never seen.

53
00:04:37,263 --> 00:04:40,043
The emperor could not pay his fee.

54
00:04:40,123 --> 00:04:43,481
So Orban went to the sultan, and
Mehmed asked him one question:

55
00:04:43,531 --> 00:04:46,073
can you bring down the
walls of Constantinople?

56
00:04:46,153 --> 00:04:50,767
He said: yes.

57
00:04:52,217 --> 00:04:56,173
In Edirne Orban cast a monstrous
cannon, about eight metres long,

58
00:04:56,223 --> 00:04:59,367
hurling stone balls weighing
hundreds of kilograms.

59
00:04:59,447 --> 00:05:04,007
Dozens of oxen and hundreds of men dragged
it for long weeks toward Constantinople.

60
00:05:04,087 --> 00:05:10,697
The old world was rolling toward its end…

61
00:05:10,777 --> 00:05:15,367
on wheels.

62
00:05:21,817 --> 00:05:30,817
In early April 1453, the plains
before the walls filled with tents.

63
00:05:30,897 --> 00:05:36,287
Modern historians estimate the
Ottoman army at some eighty thousand,

64
00:05:36,337 --> 00:05:40,097
and the defenders at only
about seven thousand.

65
00:05:40,177 --> 00:05:49,197
Among them was a commander from Genoa with
seven hundred fighters: Giovanni Giustiniani…

66
00:05:49,277 --> 00:05:53,447
the city's last hope.

67
00:05:53,527 --> 00:05:55,704
The Ottoman army spread along the walls,

68
00:05:55,754 --> 00:05:58,655
and the sultan's red tent
was pitched facing the Gate

69
00:05:58,705 --> 00:06:01,767
of Saint Romanus, where the
fiercest fighting would be.

70
00:06:03,217 --> 00:06:09,487
The Theodosian walls were three
lines: a wide moat, an outer wall,

71
00:06:09,537 --> 00:06:14,517
and a higher inner wall
guarded by dozens of towers.

72
00:06:14,597 --> 00:06:19,332
On the sea side, the Romans
stretched a massive iron chain

73
00:06:19,382 --> 00:06:24,333
across the mouth of the Golden
Horn, so no ship could enter.

74
00:06:25,783 --> 00:06:29,813
On the sixth of April,
the bombardment began.

75
00:06:29,893 --> 00:06:32,384
For the first time in
history, thousand-year-old

76
00:06:32,434 --> 00:06:34,603
walls shook under the blows of gunpowder.

77
00:06:34,683 --> 00:06:38,341
By day the shot broke
the stones; by night the

78
00:06:38,391 --> 00:06:42,663
defenders plugged the breaches
with timber and earth.

79
00:06:42,743 --> 00:06:44,813
This went on for weeks.

80
00:06:44,893 --> 00:06:50,062
The great cannon needed
hours to cool between shots,

81
00:06:50,112 --> 00:06:55,933
so the men poured oil on it
and patiently loaded it again.

82
00:07:00,383 --> 00:07:05,487
On the twentieth of April,
four ships appeared

83
00:07:05,537 --> 00:07:10,243
on the horizon, carrying aid for the city.

84
00:07:10,323 --> 00:07:14,017
The Ottoman fleet swooped on them,
but the tall ships forced their

85
00:07:14,067 --> 00:07:17,753
way through and entered the Golden
Horn to cheers from the walls.

86
00:07:17,833 --> 00:07:22,733
It is said the sultan rode his
horse into the sea in fury.

87
00:07:27,183 --> 00:07:31,313
The chain protected the city's
weakest wall: the Golden Horn wall.

88
00:07:31,393 --> 00:07:32,903
If the ships could not cross the sea…

89
00:07:32,983 --> 00:07:37,723
let them cross the land.

90
00:07:37,803 --> 00:07:41,300
An idea that seemed to
everyone like madness.

91
00:07:42,750 --> 00:07:46,213
On the night of the twenty-second of April,

92
00:07:46,263 --> 00:07:50,920
greased wooden planks were
laid over the hills of Galata.

93
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:56,024
Men and oxen hauled some
seventy ships up and down,

94
00:07:56,074 --> 00:08:00,750
their sails unfurled as
if sailing on dry land.

95
00:08:00,830 --> 00:08:02,810
When morning came, the people of
the city saw the Ottoman fleet…

96
00:08:02,890 --> 00:08:07,690
inside the Golden Horn.

97
00:08:07,770 --> 00:08:11,700
The sultan had crossed the
chain without touching it.

98
00:08:16,150 --> 00:08:23,720
In May, the war moved underground.

99
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:28,390
The Ottomans dug tunnels
under the walls, the defenders

100
00:08:28,440 --> 00:08:33,080
dug counter-tunnels, and the
two sides met in the dark.

101
00:08:33,160 --> 00:08:36,150
The Ottomans built a great wooden siege tower;
the Romans burned it in a single night.

102
00:08:36,230 --> 00:08:39,267
The siege dragged on, and
both sides were worn out.

103
00:08:40,717 --> 00:08:44,747
On the twenty-fourth of
May, the moon was eclipsed.

104
00:08:44,827 --> 00:08:47,924
Byzantine sources tell that
the icon of the Virgin slipped

105
00:08:47,974 --> 00:08:50,855
from its bearers' hands during
a procession of prayer,

106
00:08:50,905 --> 00:08:52,967
and that a thick fog covered the city.

107
00:08:53,047 --> 00:08:56,147
Despair crept into hearts…

108
00:08:56,227 --> 00:09:00,467
before it crept over the walls.

109
00:09:01,917 --> 00:09:06,242
The sultan sent the emperor
an offer of surrender: safety

110
00:09:06,292 --> 00:09:10,897
for the people, and for him,
leave to go wherever he wished.

111
00:09:10,977 --> 00:09:17,583
The reply came, as the sources relate: it
is not in my power, nor in anyone's here,

112
00:09:17,633 --> 00:09:23,567
to surrender the city; we have all
resolved to die rather than give it up.

113
00:09:25,017 --> 00:09:26,907
So Mehmed gathered his commanders.

114
00:09:26,987 --> 00:09:37,657
Halil Pasha said: lift the siege
before Europe comes to their rescue.

115
00:09:37,737 --> 00:09:43,137
Zaganos Pasha said: no, we attack
now; the city is exhausted.

116
00:09:43,217 --> 00:09:43,707
The sultan was silent for a long time…

117
00:09:43,757 --> 00:09:46,133
then he gave his word: a general assault,
at dawn on the twenty-ninth of May.

118
00:09:47,583 --> 00:09:51,633
On the twenty-eighth of
May, everything fell still.

119
00:09:51,713 --> 00:09:56,553
In the Ottoman camp the soldiers
fasted, prayed and lit fires.

120
00:09:56,633 --> 00:09:58,043
In the city, people gathered in
Hagia Sophia for the last prayer.

121
00:09:58,123 --> 00:10:02,043
Two cities praying on the same night…

122
00:10:02,123 --> 00:10:07,100
and waiting for dawn.

123
00:10:14,550 --> 00:10:18,850
Just before dawn on the
twenty-ninth of May, drums and

124
00:10:18,900 --> 00:10:23,250
horns thundered, and the
assault began in three waves.

125
00:10:23,330 --> 00:10:27,328
The first wave rushed at the
breaches, then the second,

126
00:10:27,378 --> 00:10:31,500
and the defenders threw them
back with stubborn despair.

127
00:10:31,580 --> 00:10:34,770
The third wave remained…

128
00:10:34,850 --> 00:10:39,250
the Janissaries.

129
00:10:39,330 --> 00:10:41,410
Fire, gunpowder and screams
filled the darkness,

130
00:10:41,460 --> 00:10:44,567
as the sultan watched the great breach
near the Gate of Saint Romanus.

131
00:10:49,017 --> 00:10:54,196
The Janissaries advanced in ordered
ranks to the beat of the drums,

132
00:10:54,246 --> 00:10:57,837
the sultan himself urging
them on at the moat.

133
00:10:57,917 --> 00:11:04,507
At that moment Giustiniani was gravely
wounded and carried from his post.

134
00:11:04,587 --> 00:11:09,767
When his men saw him withdraw,
panic spread through the ranks.

135
00:11:15,217 --> 00:11:20,047
It is said that a small gate
in the wall, the Kerkoporta,

136
00:11:20,097 --> 00:11:24,807
had been left open, and some
attackers slipped through.

137
00:11:24,887 --> 00:11:29,830
Ottoman sources tell of a giant
soldier named Hasan of Ulubat

138
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:35,037
who climbed the wall and raised
the banner, then fell a martyr.

139
00:11:35,117 --> 00:11:37,117
And within moments…

140
00:11:37,197 --> 00:11:39,900
banners were flying from the towers.

141
00:11:44,350 --> 00:11:49,136
As for Constantine, the accounts say
he cast off his imperial insignia

142
00:11:49,186 --> 00:11:54,230
and charged to where the fighting was
fiercest, and was never seen again.

143
00:11:54,310 --> 00:12:00,333
With him died the last trace of an empire
that had lived more than a thousand years.

144
00:12:04,783 --> 00:12:12,013
At noon Mehmed rode into the
city and made for Hagia Sophia.

145
00:12:12,093 --> 00:12:13,598
It is said he dismounted at
its door, took a handful

146
00:12:13,648 --> 00:12:15,203
of earth and scattered it
on his turban in humility.

147
00:12:15,283 --> 00:12:20,703
Then he ordered that it become a mosque.

148
00:12:20,783 --> 00:12:23,023
He was twenty-one…

149
00:12:23,103 --> 00:12:28,283
and from that day he was
called: the Conqueror.

150
00:12:28,363 --> 00:12:32,533
He led the first Friday prayer
there three days later.

151
00:12:36,983 --> 00:12:41,473
The Conqueror did not want a
ruined city, but a living capital.

152
00:12:41,553 --> 00:12:47,302
He moved his capital there, invited
people of every faith to settle,

153
00:12:47,352 --> 00:12:54,003
restored the Orthodox their patriarch,
and built mosques, markets and schools.

154
00:12:54,083 --> 00:12:56,873
And Constantinople became…

155
00:12:56,953 --> 00:13:04,353
Istanbul.

156
00:13:04,433 --> 00:13:06,296
He issued pledges of safety to
other faiths for their lives,

157
00:13:06,346 --> 00:13:08,100
churches and property, and
many who had fled came home.

158
00:13:09,550 --> 00:13:14,410
And he did not stop.

159
00:13:14,490 --> 00:13:18,951
He took Serbia, the Morea,
Trebizond and Bosnia,

160
00:13:19,001 --> 00:13:26,050
and sent his armies as far as Otranto
in southern Italy, and Rome trembled.

161
00:13:26,130 --> 00:13:30,024
On the third of May 1481 he died
on the road to a new campaign

162
00:13:30,074 --> 00:13:33,700
whose target he never revealed,
at the age of forty-nine.

163
00:13:35,150 --> 00:13:42,110
Many historians regard the fall of
Constantinople as the end of the Middle Ages.

164
00:13:42,190 --> 00:13:46,788
Gunpowder had proved that walls
no longer protected anyone,

165
00:13:46,838 --> 00:13:53,220
and Greek scholars carried their books to
Italy and helped spark its Renaissance.

166
00:13:53,300 --> 00:13:56,220
A city fell…

167
00:13:56,300 --> 00:13:58,710
and a new world began.

168
00:13:58,790 --> 00:14:04,333
Trade routes shifted, and Europe turned to
the sea in search of new routes to the East.

169
00:14:05,783 --> 00:14:09,400
A young man of twenty-one,
whom everyone underestimated,

170
00:14:09,450 --> 00:14:13,443
took what commanders had failed
to take for a thousand years.

171
00:14:13,523 --> 00:14:16,427
He did not take it by force alone,
but by knowledge and planning,

172
00:14:16,477 --> 00:14:18,203
and the daring of one who sees a road…

173
00:14:18,283 --> 00:14:23,400
where others see only a wall.

174
00:14:24,850 --> 00:14:28,490
On the shores of the Bosphorus his
fortress still stands over the strait,

175
00:14:28,540 --> 00:14:31,270
and his name still echoes on
every bridge and minaret.

176
00:14:31,350 --> 00:14:38,950
Walls fall…

177
00:14:39,030 --> 00:14:40,230
but great dreams remain.

178
00:14:41,733 --> 00:14:48,733
THE CONQUEROR
